WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Hi,
I'm Anthony Bushong,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.600
developer advocate at Google.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.820
And welcome to GKE Essentials.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.264
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.940
Did you know that GKE clusters
can scale up to 15,000 nodes?

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.580
Owning a cluster with that
sheer scale is hard for me

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.210
to comprehend at times.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.200
But let's dive into it together.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.590
This video is the first part
of a two-part introduction

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.770
to building large-scale
GKE clusters.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.720
Over the course of
these videos, we'll

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.640
talk at a high level
around two key areas--

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.180
one, why you might
consider building clusters

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.827
with thousands of
nodes, and two,

00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:43.410
what considerations
you should account

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.980
for when designing clusters
to be able to scale.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.290
Kubernetes especially
shines for those

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.900
of you looking to build a
platform to run your company's

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.310
workloads.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.290
It gives you a means
for abstracting away

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.520
infrastructure.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.590
No need to directly
deploy workloads

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.330
to a specific machine.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.340
Rather, you deploy
it to the cluster,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.210
and Kubernetes will make
sure it runs somewhere.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.250
It also gives you
declarative APIs

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.190
that codify a workload's
characteristics

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.810
and enforces that behavior.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.950
You can even extend these
APIs with your own definitions

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.560
of how workloads should run.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.690
This is why one of the
founders of Kubernetes

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.150
went as far as calling
the open-source technology

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.600
a platform for
building platforms.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.310
Now, these platforms
that you build

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.060
could be home to a set of large,
resource-intensive workloads,

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.210
such as batch processing
or machine learning.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.360
Or they could also be the
generalized way in which you

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.090
enable developers to
access infrastructure

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.070
for their various and
potentially differing

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.552
workloads.

00:01:45.552 --> 00:01:47.010
It should be noted
that when you're

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.260
building multi-tenant
clusters, the harder

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.780
isolation or stronger
boundaries that you require

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.090
between these workloads,
the more likely

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.860
it is you should consider using
multiple smaller clusters.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.980
Nevertheless, using fewer
large-scale clusters

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.740
in some of these cases can
help in a handful of ways.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.120
One, they can help you
minimize the number of clusters

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.490
you have to actively manage.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.010
This helps with problems
like configuration drift

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.550
across clusters or juggling
multiple concurrent upgrade

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.590
cycles.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.850
Two, you can more easily
handle resource spikes.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.950
If you're building
a platform, you

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.050
may have many different
teams as users,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.210
each with their own
plans for rolling out

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.070
their own workloads
to the platform

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.650
or scaling up their workloads.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.350
Building a cluster that
can scale to larger sizes

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:42.420
can help absorb these
varying demands concurrently.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.780
And finally, for those of you
building specialized platforms

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.850
for batch processing
or machine learning,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.640
building a large-scale
cluster brings the simplicity

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.520
of the Kubernetes
API to these complex,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.640
performance-intensive workloads.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.890
Even if you're not looking
to build a cluster that's

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.460
15,000 nodes, you can still
realize these benefits

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.860
by building a cluster
that can scale to house

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.700
large amounts of resources.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.370
So let's talk about
considerations

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:12.900
for designing and building a
large-scale cluster like this.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.810
When it comes down to it,
as a platform builder,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.910
you are providing a
contract to your end

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.610
users and their workloads.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.410
You ask them to
package their workload

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.260
in a container with assurances
that your platform will

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.180
run that workload reliably.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.640
So at the heart of building
large-scale clusters

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.300
is not just the concept of
adding more nodes or more pods,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.030
but rather, figuring out
how to maintain your cluster

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.300
reliability when doing so.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.330
Of course, we can't cover every
single design consideration

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.460
for large-scale clusters
in this episode.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.420
But let's dive into where
you should start thinking.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.430
It's important to
think of cluster design

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.780
across two categories.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.560
There are the considerations
at the greater Kubernetes

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.470
level, which revolves
around the Kubernetes API

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.490
and their objects, such as
pods, services, namespaces.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.740
And these are not
specific or limited

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.070
to any one
infrastructure provider.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.490
Then there are considerations
at the GKE level, which

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.610
include Google Cloud
resources, such as VPCs,

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.959
underlying compute, storage,
and GKE specific features

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.200
that help tune your cluster
to reach these larger sizes.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.250
We'll actually cover the
Google Cloud and GKE parts

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.790
in part two of this series,
so let's cover the Kubernetes

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.850
resources first.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.160
Kubernetes
scalability is defined

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.100
by the special-interest
group focused on scalability

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.860
as a multidimensional envelope.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.960
Within this envelope,
your cluster

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.350
will work and do so reliably.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.080
This envelope has
configuration limitations

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.610
at various dimensions.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.430
It could be the number of
pods, the number of namespaces,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.230
the number of secrets.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.630
So when designing a
cluster for large scale,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.730
it helps to understand
and start thinking

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:03.870
about how your cluster will
fit within this envelope.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.630
Now, I definitely don't work
with multidimensional envelopes

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.090
on a day-to-day basis,
so let's actually

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.030
talk a little bit about how
an envelope like this works.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.550
In the envelope of
Kubernetes scalability,

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.180
dimensions are not always
independent of one another.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.080
An example.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.700
In Kubernetes, we
have the ability

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.490
to run 110 pods per node.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.420
We're also able to run thousands
of nodes in a given cluster.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.370
But to do both at the same time?

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.190
This likely would
result in an unreliable

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:36.580
cluster, as you are stretching
multiple dimensions too far--

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.450
pods per node, nodes, and
total pods in the cluster.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:43.750
In a scenario like this, knowing
how these dimensions relate

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.270
to one another can help
you make adjustments,

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.000
like changing the maximum
number of pods per node

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.830
in your cluster.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.410
In GKE, for these
large-scale clusters,

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.260
we generally recommend
considering fewer pods per node

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:57.670
than the maximum.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.400
A good starting benchmark we've
seen with successful users

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.410
is roughly 30 pods per node.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.300
The next thing to
consider is, scaling

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.640
to the maximum in
any one direction

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.730
can taper your ability to scale
across the other dimension.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.630
So it's important to think about
what resources your platform

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:15.880
could push to the limit.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.590
A highly specialized
cluster to serve

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.180
as a batch-processing
platform, for example,

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.230
might have numerous pods
being created and deleted

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:26.050
at the same time, depending
on the status of jobs

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.010
that are running.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.620
And this will affect pod churn.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.510
A multi-tenant
enterprise platform

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.450
might have hundreds of workloads
across hundreds of teams,

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:38.170
each isolated to their
own set of namespaces.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.820
It's important to
plan around what

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.180
is most critical to make
your platform on Kubernetes

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:47.590
work for your users and to test
those dimensions one at a time

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:48.850
and independently.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:52.300
Finally, all the resources your
platform uses in Kubernetes

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.360
share an upper bound, which
is set by the underlying state

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.180
system for Kubernetes, etcd.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.220
This not only includes
the Kubernetes objects

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:03.160
you're probably familiar
with-- deployments, secrets,

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.150
stateful sets, et cetera--

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:08.350
but also the resources that are
created using custom resource

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:09.550
definitions.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.500
In fact, many
open-source technologies

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.780
built atop Kubernetes
utilize this

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.600
to persist their own objects
through the Kubernetes control

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.220
plane that their own set of
controllers interact with.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.950
All of these objects are
subject to etcd's underlying

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:28.240
limitations, such as 800
mebibytes per object type

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.330
and the overall total
storage of etcd.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.520
Given this, we
recommend understanding

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.500
the implications of
what is actually getting

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.690
created in your cluster.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.930
For example, the built-in
Kubernetes job resource

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.780
requires certain configurations
to ensure that resources

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.840
are cleaned up upon completion.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.480
Custom resources can
also expand one resource

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:53.500
into several others, such
as pipeline runs Tekton.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:54.670
These are not bad things.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.790
If you need to run Kubernetes
jobs or custom resources,

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:00.160
like what Tekton
provides, great.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.040
But it's important to know
their behavior when building

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.680
a platform for your end users.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.560
Now that we've taken a
high-level walkthrough

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.900
of Kubernetes
scalability, we'll need

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.020
to cover how to design around
GKE features and Google Cloud

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:17.500
infrastructure to support these
large-scale clusters, which

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:18.850
we'll cover in part two.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:21.280
In the meantime, take a
look at these resources

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.140
to better understand
the Kubernetes

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.350
scalability dimensions
that we discussed today.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.240
Thank you for your time,
and we will see you

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.910
on the next episode, part
two in GKE Essentials.

